<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The app's Info.plist base. Xcode still GENERATES the ordinary keys (bundle id,
  version, scene manifest) and still merges every INFOPLIST_KEY_* build setting
  from gen_xcodeproj.py on top of this file; what lives here is the part that
  build settings cannot express, plus the privacy strings, which are grouped
  with it so the whole TCC surface can be read in one place.

  Why this file exists at all: on visionOS a missing usage description is not a
  refused permission, it is a PROCESS KILL. The system sends SIGKILL and writes
  a TCC-namespace crash report, so it presents as "the app died instantly for no
  reason" rather than as a permissions problem — and none of it reproduces in
  the Simulator, which does not enforce TCC. That cost a run to find:

      Klepton-2026-08-07-161125.ips
      namespace TCC — "This app has crashed because it attempted to access
      privacy-sensitive data without a usage description. The app's Info.plist
      must contain an NSAccessoryTrackingUsageDescription key..."

  Note WHICH key that was. We declared hand tracking, which is the one that
  looks like it needs consent, and were killed for accessory tracking — which
  KleptonControllers requests even with zero controllers paired, because it
  builds an AccessoryTrackingProvider from an empty list. The lesson is that
  the trigger is *requesting* the authorization, not obtaining data, so the key
  set has to match the providers we construct, not the data we end up using.

  The set below is taken from ../VisionOSALVRClient/ALVRClient/Info.plist — the
  user's own visionOS client, which runs on this same headset and has already
  paid for this discovery once. Taking the whole set rather than adding them one
  kill at a time is deliberate.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- THE SCENE MANIFEST — and specifically the immersive-space ROLE.
	     Without this the app has no immersive scene session, and the failure is
	     silent in every direction that can be checked from inside the app:
	     openImmersiveSpace returns .opened, a CompositorLayer is created, its
	     LayerRenderer reaches .running, queryDrawable(s) hands back a drawable
	     with correct textures, slices, viewports and depth range, deviceAnchor
	     is accepted, command buffers complete without error, and every frame
	     presents. Nothing composites, because there is no immersive scene to
	     composite into.
	     Xcode generates the rest of this dictionary, but it generated
	     UISceneConfigurations as an EMPTY dict — the window role only. That is
	     what a `plutil -extract UIApplicationSceneManifest` on the built app
	     showed, next to Apple's MetalImmersiveTemplate which declares the role
	     below. It cost a session: the entire compositor was verified correct
	     against a display that could never show it. -->
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationPreferredDefaultSceneSessionRole</key>
		<string>UIWindowSceneSessionRoleApplication</string>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
		<true/>
		<key>UISceneConfigurations</key>
		<dict>
			<key>UISceneSessionRoleImmersiveSpaceApplication</key>
			<array>
				<dict>
					<!-- Mixed, matching the app's default (Immersive.mixed).
					     Declaring one style here and selecting another in
					     SwiftUI is a mismatch worth avoiding: it is not obviously
					     fatal, but this file's whole reason for existing is that
					     scene declarations which disagree with the app fail
					     silently. KL_FULL=1 selects .full at runtime and the
					     scene accepts both (`in: .mixed, .full`). -->
					<key>UISceneInitialImmersionStyle</key>
					<string>UIImmersionStyleMixed</string>
				</dict>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</dict>

	<!-- ARKit providers we construct in KleptonCompositor.runARKit(). All three
	     are required: worldTracking is unconditional, handTracking is added
	     whenever the platform supports it, and the accessory provider is built
	     even when no Sense controllers are paired. -->
	<key>NSWorldSensingUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Klepton reads the headset's own pose, which is the head pose the guest renders from.</string>
	<key>NSHandsTrackingUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Klepton maps your hands onto the guest's two Touch controllers.</string>
	<key>NSAccessoryTrackingUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Klepton maps PSVR2 Sense controllers onto the guest's two Touch controllers.</string>
	<!-- Sense controllers reach us through GameController. ALVR carries the
	     Bluetooth string for controllers the system does not surface as
	     standard gamepads; declared here for the same reason the rest are —
	     the cost of a missing one is a kill, and the cost of a spare is a
	     string nobody reads. -->
	<key>NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Klepton connects to spatial controllers that are not visible to the system as standard game controllers.</string>

	<!-- The LOCAL network, which on this platform is a separate permission from
	     "the network" and fails in the worst available way without it.
	     (SL-19.)

	     Steam Link finds hosts by UDP broadcast on port 27036 — SL-5 measured
	     that, and measured it colliding with a Mac running Steam. On iOS 14 and
	     later, and on visionOS, an app that has not declared this key gets no
	     prompt and its local-network traffic is DISCARDED: no errno, no error
	     on the socket, nothing in any log. That is why the device run shows
	     `Connected to Remote Client service` — the Steam backend is on the
	     internet and needs none of this — while the computer list stays empty.

	     Same class as trap 13, one step quieter: there a missing usage string
	     is a process kill on the request, here it is silence. So the failure to
	     look for is "the feature does nothing", not "the feature errors".

	     No NSBonjourServices: this guest does not browse mDNS, it broadcasts.
	     A service type we invented would be a declaration we cannot back up. -->
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Steam Link finds the PCs on your network by broadcasting to them, and streams from the one you pair with.</string>

	<!-- Array-valued, so it cannot be an INFOPLIST_KEY_ build setting — which
	     is the reason this file exists rather than four more settings. Without
	     it the Sense controllers are not offered to the app as spatial
	     gamepads at all. -->
	<key>GCSupportedGameControllers</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>ProfileName</key>
			<string>SpatialGamepad</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>ProfileName</key>
			<string>ExtendedGamepad</string>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!-- The run's whole log is Documents/klepton-boot.log, and this makes it
	     reachable from the Files app as well as from devicectl — worth having
	     on a headset that is often not tethered to the Mac that built it. -->
	<key>UIFileSharingEnabled</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
